// A segment past the edge while the connection is still SynReceived is answered at once with the edge restated. To a
// scaling peer that ACK states 65535 rounded up to the unit of 8, (65535 + 7) >> 3 = 8192, so the recorded edge stands
// at 1 + 65536, one byte past the SYN-ACK's. The SYN-ACK sent again, after the peer's SYN again and by its timer,
// still states 65535 unscaled and leaves the recorded edge where it stands: it never moves left. Once the handshake
// ends the first ACK of data states the budget as after any passive open, (370689 - 1001) / 8 = 46211. Linux answers
// such a segment from its request socket with the window shifted down rather than rounded up, so its edge does not
// run ahead of the SYN-ACK's; its windows depend on its buffers.
0 socket(..., SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_TCP) = 3
+0 bind(3, ..., ...) = 0
+0 listen(3, 1) = 0

+0 < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK,TS val 100 ecr 0,nop,wscale 7>
+0 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK,TS val 0 ecr 100,nop,wscale 3>
+0 < . 70001:70001(0) ack 1 win 512 <nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 0>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 8192 <nop,nop,TS val 0 ecr 100>
+0 < S 0:0(0) win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK,TS val 100 ecr 0,nop,wscale 7>
+0 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK,TS val 0 ecr 100,nop,wscale 3>
+1 > S. 0:0(0) ack 1 win 65535 <mss 1460,sackOK,TS val 1000 ecr 100,nop,wscale 3>
+0 < . 1:1(0) ack 1 win 512 <nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 1000>
+0 accept(3, ..., ...) = 4

+0 < P. 1:1001(1000) ack 1 win 512 <nop,nop,TS val 100 ecr 1000>
+0 > . 1:1(0) ack 1001 win 46211 <nop,nop,TS val 1000 ecr 100>
